b Z^3)\rtimes\langle x,y,z\rangle$, a normal subgroup
+of $B$.  Thus, up to conjugacy, $[d,b]$ depends only on the class of
+$b\in B$ modulo $tBt^{-1}$.  The mod-$2$ calculation in the proof of
+Theorem~\ref{thm:signfree} shows that the linear subgroup, which also
+centralizes $d$, is transitive on the seven nonzero parity classes.
+Consequently every commutator $[d,b]$ defining $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ is
+either trivial or conjugate to $u$, while $u$ is one of the defining
+commutators.  Hence $N_{\mathrm{conj}}=D$.
+
+In $E/D$ the involution $d$ centralizes $B$, and $c=t^{-1}dt$.  The
+relations $[c,B]=1$ become $[d,tBt^{-1}]=1$ and are redundant because
+$tBt^{-1}\le B$; the relations involving $w$ are redundant because
+$w=u^2=1$.  Therefore
+\[
+ E/D\cong
+ \langle B,t,d\mid tbt^{-1}=\alpha(b),\ d^2=1,\ [d,B]=1\rangle
+ \cong \Sigma *_B(B\times C_2).
+\]
+The group $\Sigma$ is the finitely generated linear group described in
+Section~\ref{sec:witness}, so it is residually finite by Mal'cev's
+theorem \cite{Malcev}.  Hence $G_0=\Sigma\times C_2$ is MF.  Shulman's
+symmetric-amalgam theorem implies that the group double
+$G_0*_B G_0$ is MF \cite[Theorem~10]{Shulman}.  The natural map
+\[
+ \Sigma *_B(B\times C_2)\longrightarrow G_0*_B G_0
+\]
+is injective by the normal-form theorem for amalgamated free products:
+a syllable in $\Sigma\setminus B$ stays outside $B$ in the first
+factor, and a syllable in $(B\times C_2)\setminus B$ stays outside
+$B$ in the second.  Thus $E/D$ is MF.
+
+The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:signfree}, together with
+Corollary~\ref{cor:pullback}, gives $u\in
+\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$ and hence
+$D\le\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$.  Corollary~\ref{cor:exactradical}
+now gives $\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)=D$.
+Applying Corollary~\ref{cor:pullback} to
+$q\colon E\to E/\langle w\rangle$ yields
+\[
+ \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E/\langle w\rangle)
+ =q(D)=D/\langle w\rangle.
+\]
+The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:signfree} also shows that $q(d)$ is an
+involutive orbit witness for $(\bar B,\bar t)$, so the normal closure
+of $q(u)$ is contained in $D_{\mathrm{coll}}$.  Hence
+$D/\langle w\rangle\le D_{\mathrm{coll}}$, while
+Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} gives the reverse containment through the
+MF residual.  This proves the remaining equalities.
+
+
+\paragraph{A torsion-free finitely presented example.}
+There exists a two-generated finitely presented torsion-free group
+$Q$ with property~\textup{(T)} such that
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:full-mf-residual}
+ \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(Q)=Q.
+\end{equation}
+Consequently every homomorphism from $Q$ to an MF group is trivial,
+and every nontrivial quotient of $Q$ has full MF residual and is
+non-MF.
+
+Use the construction of Fournier-Facio \cite[\S2]{FFF}, choosing the
+common quotient there as in Hull's Corollary~7.4 \cite{Hull}.  It gives
+a finitely presented torsion-free acylindrically hyperbolic group $G$
+with property~\textup{(T)}, a property~\textup{(T)} subgroup
+$\Gamma\le G$, a finitely presented infinite simple torsion-free
+subgroup $S\le\Gamma$, and an element $t\in G$ such that
+\[
+ t\Gamma t^{-1}\le\Gamma,
+ \qquad
+ t^{-1}St\subseteq C_G(\Gamma).
+\]
+Choose $1\ne s\in S$ and put $c=t^{-1}st$.  If
+$\iota\colon\Gamma\hookrightarrow G$ denotes the inclusion, then
+$(\Gamma,\iota,t,c)$ is a Kazhdan conjugation datum and
+$tct^{-1}=s$.  Since $S$ is nonabelian, choose $r\in S$ with
+$[s,r]\ne1$.  This commutator belongs to $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$, and
+$N_{\mathrm{conj}}\cap S$ is a nontrivial normal subgroup of the simple
+group $S$.  Thus
+\[
+ R:=\langle\!\langle S\rangle\!\rangle_G
+ \le N_{\mathrm{conj}}.
+\]
+
+The subgroup $R$ is infinite and normal.  It is therefore $s$-normal,
+so Osin's Lemma~7.2 makes its action non-elementary for a
+non-elementary acylindrical action of $G$ \cite{Osin}.  Since $G$ is
+torsion-free, it has no nontrivial finite subgroups, and hence $R$ is
+suitable in Hull's sense.  Hull's Corollary~5.7 and Lemma~5.8 provide
+a two-generated suitable subgroup
+$S_0=\langle h_1,h_2\rangle\le R$ \cite{Hull}.
+
+Let $g_1,\ldots,g_m$ generate $G$.  Apply Hull's Theorem~7.1 to $S_0$
+and the finite set $\{g_1,\ldots,g_m\}$, choosing the protected ball
+large enough to contain $s$.  We obtain a quotient
+$p\colon G\twoheadrightarrow Q$ such that $p(s)\ne1$, every $p(g_i)$
+lies in $p(S_0)$, and no new torsion is created.  Hence
+\[
+ Q=p(S_0)=p(R),
+\]
+so $Q$ is two-generated and torsion-free.  The proof of Hull's theorem
+adds only finitely many relators for a finite prescribed set, so $Q$
+is finitely presented.  Property~\textup{(T)} passes from $G$ to $Q$.
+
+The Kazhdan conjugation datum descends through $p$.  Its conjugation
+defect contains $p(R)=Q$, and therefore its $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ is all
+of $Q$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan}, applied with the normal
+Kazhdan subgroup $K=Q$, gives \eqref{eq:full-mf-residual}.  Finally,
+Lemma~\ref{lem:portable} shows that the image of the MF residual under
+any quotient map lies in the MF residual of the quotient, so every
+quotient of $Q$ again has full residual.
+
+
+\begin{remark}[the torsion-free realization]\label{rem:ff-realization}
+The Clifford construction of Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-clifford}
+itself cannot produce a torsion-free group: it imposes $c^2=1$, hence
+$\hat c^2=1$, and every homomorphism to a torsion-free group kills
+$\hat c$ and its conjugation defect.  The preceding construction instead
+uses the infinite-order element $c=t^{-1}st$ from
+Fournier-Facio's construction and then makes its normal defect generate
+the quotient by Hull's small-cancellation theorem.
 \end{remark}
 
+\paragraph{Building blocks beyond matrices.}
